\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The two physical architectures at one scale, drawn from the solver mesh (layer heights measured, not typed). Z1: the converter is embedded between substrate and die, and its 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V arrives through the ball-grid array and the package 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V redistribution layer. Z2: the converter hangs below a 300\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}$\mu $m substrate inside a 23.36\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm opening of the 2.4\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm board, with a 1.0\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm void between the cell grid and the opening sidewall; its 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V enters through an illustrative direct feed (the exact connector geometry is not specified), and only 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V crosses the substrate through the pillar attach and the TSV fields. The BGA balls of Z2 carry signals, auxiliary rails and mechanical support, not the 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V input.}}{4}{figure.2}}
\newlabel{fig:package}{{2}{4}{The two physical architectures at one scale, drawn from the solver mesh (layer heights measured, not typed). Z1: the converter is embedded between substrate and die, and its 48\,V arrives through the ball-grid array and the package 48\,V redistribution layer. Z2: the converter hangs below a \SubZtwoUM \,$\mu $m substrate inside a \CutoutMM \,mm opening of the 2.4\,mm board, with a \ClearanceMM \,mm void between the cell grid and the opening sidewall; its 48\,V enters through an illustrative direct feed (the exact connector geometry is not specified), and only 1\,V crosses the substrate through the pillar attach and the TSV fields. The BGA balls of Z2 carry signals, auxiliary rails and mechanical support, not the 48\,V input}{figure.2}{}}
